INT. ICE-FISHING SHANTY - LAKE SUPERIOR - NIGHT

A plywood box on a frozen lake, six by eight, glowing orange from a propane heater. Four holes drilled in the floor, four lines dropped into black water. Thermoses, a deck of cards, a battered radio playing country music.

HOLLIS MCINTYRE, 50s, flannel, beard iced at the tips, sits on an overturned bucket. MARGOT LEBLANC, 40s, wool cap, jigs her rod in slow circles. TEDDY ORLOV, 12, her nephew, in an oversized snowmobile suit, stares into his hole. BRIGID FALLON, 30s, newcomer, borrowed boots, holds a cup of cocoa with both hands. ANSEL, 70s, Hollis's father, dozes by the door.

HOLLIS
Coldest night of the year, they said.

MARGOT
Warmest, if you're lucky.

BRIGID
Do they bite when it's like this?

HOLLIS
They bite when they feel like it.

TEDDY
I got one!

All four turn. Teddy's line goes taut. He heaves.

MARGOT
Easy, kiddo!

HOLLIS
Let him fight!

The rod bends nearly double. Ice creaks. Then the line goes slack. Teddy tumbles back, laughing.

TEDDY
It got away!

BRIGID
That's the best part.

She sips. Settles. The heater hisses. Ansel snores.

MARGOT
I love this. The quiet.

HOLLIS
It's not that quiet.

MARGOT
It's quiet enough.

For a minute, no one speaks. The radio plays a slow song. Snow ticks on the roof.

BRIGID
Does the lake ever talk?

HOLLIS
Talk?

BRIGID
The ice. It was making a noise earlier. Like a groan.

MARGOT
It does that. Expansion.

HOLLIS
It's the cold working through it.

The groan comes again. Longer, lower. Teddy leans closer to the hole.

TEDDY
It sounds like a person.

MARGOT
Teddy.

TEDDY
Like someone humming.

HOLLIS
Don't be silly.

MARGOT ^
Don't be silly.

They've said it at the same time, same flat tone. They look at each other and neither smiles.

The humming continues. Not the radio. From under the floor.

Ansel's eyes open.

ANSEL
(whispering)
Nobody put your hand in the hole.

BRIGID
What?

ANSEL
Don't. Please.

Teddy's fingers are already at the edge of the water.

From the black circle, a pale hand rises, slow, almost tender. It takes Teddy's mitten between two fingers, holds it, as if checking the size.

The heater goes out.
